<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Said</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1935">1935</a>–<a href="/years/2003">2003</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Edward Wadie Said</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Said was a Palestinian and American academic, literary critic and political activist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> under the British Mandate, who taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> from 1963 until 2003. His book Orientalism (1978), a critique of how the Western world has represented the Orient, made him one of the founders of post-colonial studies; Culture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">Imperialism</a> followed in 1993. A member of the Palestinian National Council, he resigned in 1993 over his criticism of the Oslo Accords, and in 1999 argued that lasting peace was possible only in one Israeli–Palestinian state. That year he co-founded the West-Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divan">Divan</a> Orchestra with Barenboim. He died of leukemia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City in 2003.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-said.jpg" alt="Edward Said" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Barenboim-Said Akademie gGmbH · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Said_and_Daniel_Barenboim_in_Sevilla,_2002_Crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edward Said was born on 1 November 1935 into a Protestant Palestinian Christian family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, then under the British Mandate. His father, Wadie Said, a businessman, had served in the American Expeditionary Forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and so earned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> citizenship for himself and his family. His mother, Hilda, was half Palestinian and half Lebanese and was raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a>. His father had founded a stationery business in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1919. After the war of 1948 the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where Said attended Victoria College, and then to the United States, where he attended Northfield Mount Hermon School. He graduated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeton-university">Princeton University</a> in 1957 and received a doctorate in English literature from Harvard in 1964.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Said's first book, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-conrad">Joseph Conrad</a> and the Fiction of Autobiography (1966), grew out of his doctoral dissertation, and Beginnings: Intention and Method (1974) examined the theoretical bases of literary criticism. His study Orientalism (1978) argued that much Western study of Islamic civilisation served the self-affirmation of European identity rather than objective scholarship, and that Oriental studies thereby worked as a method of cultural discrimination and imperial domination. It proposed a persistent Eurocentric prejudice against Arabo-Islamic peoples, rooted in a long tradition of romanticised and false images of the Orient. Said wrote that such images had served as implicit justification for the colonial ambitions of the European powers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>He later wrote The World, the Text, and the Critic (1983), Culture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">Imperialism</a> (1993) and the Reith Lectures of 1993, published as Representations of the Intellectual. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967 he became a public intellectual by challenging how American news media explained the Arab-Israeli conflict, and he wrote The Question of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> (1979). In the essay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zionism">Zionism</a> from the Standpoint of Its Victims he argued for the legitimacy of the claim to a Jewish homeland while asserting the Palestinian people's equal right of self-determination.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Said was also an accomplished pianist and a music critic for The Nation, and wrote four books on music. With the conductor Daniel Barenboim he founded the West-Eastern Divan Orchestra in 1999, made up of young Israeli, Palestinian and Arab musicians, and the Barenboim-Said Foundation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. He lectured at more than 200 universities and received about twenty honorary degrees. He had struggled for twelve years with chronic lymphocytic leukemia when he died in 2003, and was buried in the Protestant Cemetery in Broumana in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Birzeit University renamed its music school the Edward Said National Conservatory of Music in 2004, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> has an Edward Said Chair.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Said” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scholars such as Joseph Massad, <b>Edward Said</b> and Nur Masalha describe Zionism as colonial or settler colonial, and Palestinians such as Noura Erakat and Mohammed El-Kurd see it as inseparable from the Nakba.</blockquote><a href="/movement/zionism">Zionism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Postcolonial scholarship, from <b>Edward Said</b>’s Orientalism (1978) to the Subaltern Studies historians of South Asia, has examined how colonial knowledge and culture sustained rule and how the colonised spoke and acted for themselves.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Edward Said</b> argued in Orientalism (1978) that Western scholarship built an imagined East, irrational and backward, as the ideological justification of Western imperialism, and the blank spaces of 19th-century maps of Africa gave the powers an incentive to fill them.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Core Ideas</a></li><li><blockquote>Feminist critics argue that nationalism justifies sexual control and repression, and <b>Edward Said</b> that nationalist narratives often go hand in hand with racism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jerusalem, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=1979" aria-label="Jerusalem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5458 1007 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward Said"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.78,35.23&amp;year=1979">What was here in 1979? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
